WHAT: guard everything in BackendManager that loads a plugin from disk with
      `#if QT_CONFIG(library)` -- the unload loop, the loader-dedup helper,
      QCoreApplication::libraryPaths(), and the whole dynamic-discovery loop in
      refresh(). Static plugins are untouched.
WHY:  drumstick does not build against a Qt configured without library/plugin
      loading, and that is not an exotic configuration -- it is what every
      platform with no dlopen gets, wasm included. In such a Qt, QPluginLoader
      keeps only its static half: `staticInstances()` is there, but
      `instance()`, `fileName()`, `unload()`, the QString constructor,
      QLibrary and QCoreApplication::libraryPaths() are all compiled out.
      Six hard errors in one file, and the last one is the confusing kind:
      because the lambda body fails to compile, its return type deduces to
      void and the failure is reported inside libc++'s __invoke rather than at
      drumstick's own `loader->fileName()`.
      Nothing is lost that this build could have had. A plugin loaded at
      runtime from a shared object is precisely what a component cannot do; the
      static path immediately below the guarded loop is the one that runs, and
      it is how the wk backends (and every other STATIC_DRUMSTICK backend) are
      found.
UPSTREAM: as-is. This fixes a real portability bug in their tree -- a Qt built
      with -no-feature-library is a supported Qt on any platform, and the guard
      is the idiom Qt itself uses. It is not wk-specific in any way: no
      Q_OS_WASI, no wk anything. Their own static Windows and macOS builds
      would benefit from it the moment someone turns the feature off.
